    "summary": "Spooky Dark Star and high‑octane Turn On Your Lovelight jam",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a deep, spooky Dark Star that drifts into an exhilarating Turn On Your Lovelight finale. Tom Constanten’s organ sits front‑and‑center, adding psychedelic weight to the extended jams. Overall the performance is tight and energetic, making it a solid example of late‑’69 Dead.",
    "review": "August 23, 1969 at Pelletier Farm captures the Grateful Dead in an exploratory mood that balances bluesy rockers with cosmic improvisation. The first half kicks off with a rousing \"Hard To Handle\" and swings through country‑flavored numbers like \"Mama Tried\" before launching into a dark, space‑filled \"Dark Star\" that feels both haunted and mesmerizing. From there the band rides a seamless wave of energy: \"Saint Stephen\" rolls straight into \"The Eleven,\" each song feeding the next, culminating in an exuberant \"Turn On Your Lovelight\" that showcases the group’s high‑octane jamming spirit. A notable feature of this show is the organ work – listeners hear a prominent, psychedelic texture that most agree belongs to Tom Constanten rather than Pigpen, giving the set a unique sonic character for this era. The recording quality is solid for a 1969 soundboard tape, with good clarity and enough presence to let the nuances of the performance shine through.",
